---
description: when code to be added/modified is part of a go grpc microservice
alwaysApply: false
---
  You are an expert in Go, gRPC, and scalable microservice development. You give thoughtful and nuanced answers that are accurate and factual. You write bug-free, production ready code.

  Pair with: code-craft, go-core, backend-usecases (when cross-domain orchestration applies), and monorepo rules for `protobuf/` + `backend/lib/gen/pb`. HTTP audience URL versioning (http-api-contract) does **not** apply — version gRPC via protobuf packages. Include `testing` when writing automated tests.

### Key Principles
  - Write concise, technical Go code (see also code-craft and go-core).
  - Organize by feature under `internal/features/<name>/`, not by technical layer folders.
  - Within a feature, group by fixed file responsibilities (`endpoints.go`, `service.go`, `errors.go`, `config.go`; add `repository.go` when persistence is needed; add `dto.go` / `entity.go` when types outgrow the main files).
  - Do not invent layer subfolders under a feature (`handlers/`, `repositories/`, etc.). In particular, do **not** put a `services/` directory inside a feature package — feature logic stays in that package’s `service.go`. Shared domain modules live only at `internal/services/<name>/`.
  - Package roles (do not conflate these):
    - `internal/features/<name>/` — primary unit: transport + feature-owned domain logic
    - `internal/services/<name>/` — shared application/domain services used by 2+ features (no gRPC registration here). Promote only after real reuse; do not put domain workflows in `common/`
    - `internal/integrations/<name>/` — external systems (NATS, Cognito, etc.)
    - `internal/database/` — SQL infra when needed
    - `internal/common/` — dumb helpers only, extracted after real reuse
    - `internal/usecases/<name>/` — optional; rare cross-domain orchestration RPCs (see backend-usecases)
  - Feature-only adapters may start inside the feature and move to `integrations/` when they become service-wide.
  - Single-provider integrations may start with `client.go` + `models.go` only; add `<provider>.go` when a second vendor/impl appears.
  - Features may be in-memory, messaging-backed, SQL-backed, or mixed — pick the persistence/integration that fits the domain.
  - Feature/usecase service entrypoints orchestrate by **calling** single-job helpers; do not inline separable multi-concern logic in the entrypoint (see code-craft Composition). Unit-test the helpers; cover the orchestrator with E2E/L2 (`testing.mdc`).
  - Protobufs are the API contract: define services/messages in the monorepo `protobuf/` tree; consume generated Go from the shared gen package (e.g. `backend/lib/gen/pb`). Do not hand-edit generated pb code.

### Go/gRPC
  - Keep endpoints thin and push use-case logic into feature/shared services.
  - Prefer UUID v7 for IDs (see code-craft / go-core).
  - File structure (feature module approach):

```text
grpcserver/                          # e.g. backend/bserver (or domain name such as chatserver)
├── cmd/
│   ├── server/main.go               # gRPC (and optional grpc-web) entrypoint
│   ├── client/main.go               # Optional but recommended: developer CLI to exercise the service
│   └── migrator/main.go             # Optional: only if an in-process migrator binary is needed
├── Dockerfile                       # Production build (prefer multi-stage; distroless optional)
├── Dockerfile.air                   # Optional: hot-reload local image
├── sqlc.yaml                        # Optional: only when the service uses SQL
├── docs/                            # Optional: service docs
├── testing/                         # Optional: integration / heavier tests
│   ├── integration_tests/
│   └── test_utils/
├── internal/
│   ├── server/                      # Optional early; recommended as the service grows: bootstrap, interceptors, lifecycle
│   ├── client/                      # Optional: client library used by cmd/client (and tests)
│   ├── environment/                 # Config from env (preferred over ad-hoc getEnv in main)
│   ├── config/                      # Optional: Viper/YAML loaders when multi-env needs them
│   ├── monitoring/                  # Optional: OTel traces/metrics/error helpers
│   ├── tools/                       # Optional: migrator helpers
│   ├── database/                    # Optional: SQL infra (only if DB used)
│   │   ├── migrations/
│   │   ├── queries/
│   │   └── sqlc/                    # sqlc-generated (do not hand-edit)
│   ├── integrations/                # External-system adapters (only when needed)
│   │   └── <name>/                  # e.g. network/ (NATS), llm/, cognito/
│   │       ├── client.go            # Provider-agnostic interface + wiring
│   │       ├── <provider>.go        # Optional until a second vendor appears
│   │       ├── models.go            # DTOs / request-response types for this integration
│   │       └── client_test.go       # Optional
│   ├── services/                    # Shared app/domain services (only after 2+ features need them)
│   │   └── <name>/                  # no gRPC service registration here
│   │       ├── service.go           # Business rules / workflows
│   │       ├── models.go            # Domain models / types
│   │       ├── repository.go        # Optional: when this shared service owns persistence
│   │       └── service_test.go      # Optional
│   ├── features/<feature>/          # PRIMARY unit of organization
│   │   ├── endpoints.go             # gRPC service impl (embeds Unimplemented*Server)
│   │   ├── service.go               # Feature-owned use-case logic; orchestrates repos/integrations/services
│   │   ├── service_test.go
│   │   ├── endpoints_test.go        # Optional: unit tests for endpoint mapping
│   │   ├── errors.go                # Domain / feature-level errors
│   │   ├── config.go                # Feature-specific configuration types (when needed)
│   │   ├── repository.go            # Optional: only if persistence is needed
│   │   ├── dto.go / entity.go       # Optional: split out when types grow
│   │   ├── injector.go              # Optional: Wire when DI complexity warrants it
│   │   └── injector_gen.go          # Optional: generated Wire (do not hand-edit)
│   ├── usecases/                    # Optional: rare cross-domain orchestration (see backend-usecases)
│   │   └── <name>/
│   │       ├── endpoints.go         # gRPC service impl for this product action
│   │       ├── service.go           # Orchestrates features/services/integrations only
│   │       ├── errors.go
│   │       ├── dto.go               # Optional
│   │       └── config.go            # Optional
│   └── common/                      # Dumb helpers only — after real reuse
│       └── utils/                   # shared id, enum, pointer, helpers
└── ...

# Monorepo (repo root / shared libs — not inside each gRPC service):
protobuf/                            # Shared .proto API contracts
backend/lib/gen/pb/                  # Generated Go from protobuf/ (do not hand-edit)
docker-compose.yml                   # Local infra + services
docker-compose.dev.yml               # Optional: air/hot-reload overrides
```

  - High-level `internal/` responsibilities:
    - `server/` (when present): gRPC server construction, interceptors, keepalive, graceful shutdown, registration of feature/usecase servers
    - `environment/`: env-based config loading
    - `client/`: programmatic client used by CLI and tests
    - `database/`: connection setup, migrations, sqlc (only if SQL)
    - `integrations/`: adapters for external systems the service depends on (e.g. NATS)
    - `services/`: shared domain/application logic used by multiple features (no transport)
    - `features/<name>/`: gRPC endpoints, feature-owned services, optional repositories, feature errors/config
    - `usecases/<name>/`: optional cross-domain orchestration RPCs (see backend-usecases)
    - `common/`: helpers only — never domain workflows
    - `monitoring/`, `tools/`, Wire: add when needed, not by default
  - If any file in a feature gets long (> 300 lines), split by clear responsibility within the same package — still prefer flat files over inventing layer folders.
  - DI: manual constructors in `cmd/server` or `internal/server` are the default — wire integrations, shared `services/`, feature services, and optional usecases into gRPC servers. Introduce Google Wire when wiring becomes painful.
  - When SQL is needed: prefer sqlc (see go-core); put migrations/queries/sqlc under `internal/database/`; do not hand-edit sqlc output.
  - Soft-delete and RLS: only when the domain/tenancy model requires them.
  - Prefer extracting bootstrap out of `main` into `internal/server` once startup logic grows past simple listen/register/serve.

### Use Cases

  Follow backend-usecases. Paths for this stack: `internal/usecases/<name>/` (file set shown in the tree above).

### Naming Conventions
  - Follow go-core for package-context type names, `Err` prefix, and conversion-helper placement.
  - Method names: idiomatic Go is fine (`Chat`, `GetRoom`, `ListMessages`).
  - gRPC endpoint structs: typically `Server` embedding `Unimplemented<Service>Server`.

### Error Handling and Validation
  - Follow go-core for early returns, `Err` vars, `errors.Is` / `errors.As`, and `%w` wrapping.
  - Distinguish context cancellation / deadline from unexpected failures (especially on streams).
  - Map domain errors to gRPC status codes in the endpoint layer (not deep inside integrations):

```go
return status.Errorf(codes.NotFound, "room not found")
// or: return status.Error(codes.InvalidArgument, "user id required")
```

  - Common gRPC codes: `OK`, `InvalidArgument`, `NotFound`, `AlreadyExists`, `PermissionDenied`, `Unauthenticated`, `FailedPrecondition`, `Aborted`, `ResourceExhausted`, `Unavailable`, `Canceled`, `DeadlineExceeded`, `Internal`.
  - Prefer shared interceptors for panic recovery, request logging, and auth once more than one RPC needs them.
  - Unexpected errors via monitoring helpers when those exist; otherwise log and return `codes.Internal` (avoid leaking internals to clients).

### Dependencies

  Staples (expected for most gRPC services):
  - Language / lint / tests / IDs / SQL driver+sqlc preferences: see go-core
  - gRPC: `google.golang.org/grpc`
  - Protobuf runtime: `google.golang.org/protobuf`
  - Generated API: shared module under `backend/lib/gen/pb` (from monorepo `protobuf/`)
  - Config: env via `internal/environment` by default

  When the service uses SQL (optional per service, but staples once SQL exists):
  - Migrations: golang-migrate

  Optional / service-specific (add only when needed):
  - Messaging: NATS (`nats.go`; embedded `nats-server` for local/dev or tests) — used by chat-style fan-out, not required for every gRPC service
  - Browser clients: `improbable-eng/grpc-web` (or successor) when browsers must speak to the service without a separate gateway
  - Auth: JWT / Cognito / similar interceptors only if the service needs identity
  - Observability: OpenTelemetry, richer logging (e.g. zerolog)
  - DI: Google Wire when constructor graphs hurt
  - Mocks: uber-go/mock when interface mocking helps
  - Local reload: air
  - Task runner: Just (only if the service already uses it)
  - Developer UX: `cmd/client` + `internal/client` CLI against the live service (strongly recommended for streaming services)

### gRPC-Specific Guidelines
  - Keep endpoints as thin adapters: validate/authz → call service → map errors to `status` codes.
  - Always embed `Unimplemented<Service>Server` in the server struct for forward compatibility.
  - Register services explicitly (`pb.RegisterXServer(grpcServer, impl)`).
  - Prefer `grpc.NewClient` (or current non-deprecated dial APIs) over legacy `grpc.Dial` in new code.
  - Use transport credentials appropriately: insecure only for local/dev; TLS (or mesh-provided TLS) outside that.
  - For long-lived streams, configure server/client keepalive deliberately (idle timeouts, ping interval, max connection age for rolling deploys).
  - Handle stream lifecycle explicitly: `io.EOF` (client done), `codes.Canceled` / context done, and unexpected recv/send errors.
  - Prefer context propagation end-to-end; cancel in-flight work when the RPC context ends.
  - Interceptors (unary/stream): logging, recovery, auth, timeouts — shared in `internal/server` when used.
  - Optional: gRPC Health Checking Protocol for orchestrators/load balancers.
  - grpc-web: only when browser clients need direct access; keep origin allowlists tight outside local dev.
  - Proto changes: edit `protobuf/`, regenerate into `backend/lib/gen/pb`, then update service code. Never hand-edit `*.pb.go` / `*_grpc.pb.go`.
  - Keep proto packages/versioning clear (directory + go_package) so multiple services can evolve contracts safely.
  - Local secrets in `.env` / env vars; never commit credentials.

### Protobuf and Codegen
  - Source of truth: monorepo `protobuf/**/*.proto`.
  - Generated Go lives in a shared module (e.g. `backend/lib/gen/pb`) consumed via Go module replace/version — not copied into each service.
  - Service READMEs should point at the protobuf regenerate workflow.
  - Prefer small, focused proto packages (e.g. `chat/endpoint/room`, `network/chat`) over one mega-proto.

### Database and Migrations
  - Only when the gRPC service needs SQL persistence.
  - Same conventions as HTTP Go services: `internal/database/{migrations,queries,sqlc}`, golang-migrate, sqlc generate after schema changes.
  - Migration naming: `create_<domain>_table`, `alter_<table>_<description>`.
  - Running migrate via compose image or CLI is fine; `cmd/migrator` is optional.

### DI, Mocks, and Testing
  - Default wiring: construct integrations → services → endpoint servers in `cmd/server` or `internal/server` with plain constructors.
  - Add Wire only when complexity warrants it; do not hand-edit generated Wire output.
  - Follow `testing.mdc` for what to test at each level. Tooling: see go-core (testify; bufconn / in-process gRPC for L2).
  - Unit-test single-job helpers (`*_test.go` colocated); cover orchestrators and RPC contracts via L2 (`testing/integration_tests/` + `test_utils/` when useful) or E2E.
  - Prefer injectable integrations so L2 can swap fakes/embedded servers. Avoid brittle `time.Sleep`-based synchronization when wait/signal patterns are practical.
  - `cmd/client` is for manual exploration; automated coverage belongs in tests.

### Performance and Observability
  - Minimize blocking I/O; keep DB, messaging, and stream IO context-aware and cancellable.
  - Tune message buffer sizes and subscription handlers for streaming/fan-out workloads when applicable.
  - Add OpenTelemetry / metrics when operational needs justify them.
  - Prefer multi-stage Docker builds; distroless/nonroot is a good production target when hardening images.
  - Expose ports clearly (native gRPC vs grpc-web) and document them so they are discoverable from the service README (per `documentation.mdc`).

  Key Conventions
  1. Organize by feature; keep layered responsibilities in flat files (`endpoints`, `service`, optional `repository`, `errors`, `config`).
  2. Use manual DI by default; Wire only when complexity warrants it.
  3. Put SQL under `internal/database/`; externals under `integrations/`; shared domain under `services/` (after reuse); dumb helpers under `common/`; optional cross-domain RPCs under `usecases/`.
  4. Never hand-edit generated protobuf or sqlc output.
  5. Map domain errors to gRPC `status`/`codes` at the endpoint boundary; follow go-core for `%w` / `errors.Is` internally.
  6. Embed `Unimplemented*Server`; handle stream/context cancellation explicitly.
  7. Treat NATS/grpc-web/auth/OTel/Wire/Just/SQL and `usecases/` as opt-in — required only when the service’s role needs them.
